Q. What are the benefits of converting a linked structure to a Red-Black Tree?


“The main benefit of converting a heavily-collided bucket from a linked structure into a Red-Black Tree is improved performance.

With a linked list, searching for a key in a bucket can take **O(n)** time in the worst case because entries may need to be checked one by one.

A Red-Black Tree is a self-balancing binary search tree, so searching, inserting, 
and deleting entries in that bucket can generally be performed in **O(log n)** time.

This is particularly useful when many different keys produce the same bucket index.
Instead of allowing the collision chain to grow linearly, HashMap keeps the structure balanced.

So the primary benefit is **better worst-case performance under heavy hash collisions**,
improving bucket operations from approximately **O(n)** to **O(log n)**.”


